(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-endocrine-quiz-version-7"]={"title":"Endocrine System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 7)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The pancreas is described in the lecture as approximately how long?","choices":["30–35 cm","10–12 cm","20–25 cm","15–18 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pancreas is described as approximately 20–25 cm long. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"The pancreas as an endocrine organ is supplied by fenestrated capillaries and which named arteries, in addition to the pancreatic arteries?","choices":["Pancreaticoduodenal arteries","Splenic branches only","Renal arteries","Gastroduodenal branches only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreas is supplied by the pancreaticoduodenal arteries in addition to the pancreatic arteries. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"The pancreas is located in which two abdominal regions?","choices":["Umbilical and hypogastric","Right hypochondriac and umbilical","Left and right lumbar","Epigastric and left hypochondriac"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreas is located in the epigastric and left hypochondriac regions. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Within the pancreatic islets, which endocrine cell type secretes somatostatin?","choices":["Delta cells","F cells","Beta cells","Alpha cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Delta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete somatostatin. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Within the pancreatic islets, which endocrine cell type secretes pancreatic polypeptide?","choices":["Beta cells","Delta cells","F cells","Alpha cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"F cells of the pancreatic islets secrete pancreatic polypeptide. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"The body of the pancreas crosses which organ, per the lecture's anatomical relations?","choices":["The left kidney","The right kidney","The spleen only","The liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"The body of the pancreas is described as crossing the left kidney. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Anteriorly, the pancreas is related to which structure, per the lecture?","choices":["The transverse colon","The splenic flexure","The posterior stomach wall","The duodenal loop only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreas is related anteriorly to the transverse colon. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"The uncinate process of the pancreas lies posterior to the SMA and SMV, and anterior to which structure?","choices":["The splenic artery","The left renal vein","The aorta","The celiac trunk"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uncinate process lies posterior to the SMA/SMV and anterior to the aorta. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Posteriorly, the body of the pancreas is related to the left crus, the left adrenal gland, the splenic vein, and which other vessel?","choices":["The left renal artery","The left renal vein","The superior mesenteric vein","The right renal vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The body of the pancreas is related posteriorly to the left crus, left adrenal, left renal vein, and splenic vein. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"The splenic artery, a landmark structure of the pancreas, is a branch of the celiac trunk that passes in which direction along the pancreas?","choices":["Left to right along the lower margin of the body","Anterior to posterior through the neck of the gland","Superior to inferior through the head of the pancreas","Right to left along the upper margin of the body and tail"],"answer":3,"exp":"The splenic artery passes from right to left along the upper margin of the pancreatic body and tail. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"The splenic vein, which runs on the posterior surface of the pancreas parallel to the splenic artery, terminates in which vessel?","choices":["The inferior vena cava","The superior mesenteric artery","The portal vein","The left renal vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The splenic vein terminates in the portal vein. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum, then through the head of the pancreas, terminating at the:","choices":["Duodenojejunal flexure","Ampulla of Vater","Pyloric sphincter","Duct of Santorini"],"answer":1,"exp":"The common bile duct terminates at the ampulla of Vater after passing through the head of the pancreas. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) results from the fusion during fetal development of ducts from which two embryologic pancreatic buds?","choices":["Proximal and distal pancreas","Right and left pancreas","Ventral and dorsal pancreas","Superior and inferior pancreas"],"answer":2,"exp":"The main pancreatic duct forms from the fusion of ducts from the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds during fetal development. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini), when present, joins the duodenum at which structure?","choices":["The pyloric sphincter","The major duodenal papilla","The ampulla of Vater","The minor papilla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The duct of Santorini, the accessory pancreatic duct, joins the duodenum at the minor papilla. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The superior mesenteric vein (SMV), a vascular landmark of the pancreas, has a diameter compared to the SMA that is:","choices":["Smaller than the SMA","Identical to the SMA","Variable with no consistent relationship","Larger than the SMA"],"answer":3,"exp":"The SMV has a larger diameter than the SMA, and lies to its right. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"The uncinate process of the pancreas wraps around the SMV and SMA, lying in which position relative to them?","choices":["Anterior and lateral","Superior and lateral","Inferior and lateral","Posterior and medial"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uncinate process wraps around the SMV (and SMA), lying posterior and medial to them. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Each adult suprarenal gland measures approximately what dimensions?","choices":["12 x 6 x 3 cm","5 x 3 x 1 cm","2 x 1 x 0.5 cm","8 x 5 x 2 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each adult suprarenal gland measures approximately 5 x 3 x 1 cm. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Each adult suprarenal gland weighs approximately how much?","choices":["15 grams","1 gram","30 grams","5 grams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each adult suprarenal gland weighs approximately 5 grams. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"At birth, the suprarenal gland is approximately what fraction of the size of a kidney, compared to only about 1/13 in adulthood?","choices":["1/10","1/6","1/20","1/3"],"answer":3,"exp":"At birth, the suprarenal gland is about 1/3 the size of a kidney, shrinking in relative proportion to about 1/13 by adulthood. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"The suprarenal glands are enclosed in renal fascia but are separated from the kidneys by which tissue?","choices":["Adipose tissue only","Peritoneum","Fibrous tissue","Muscle fascia"],"answer":2,"exp":"The suprarenal glands are enclosed in renal fascia but separated from the kidneys by fibrous tissue. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"In cross section, the human adrenal gland is described as having which general shape?","choices":["Bilobed","Ovoid","Perfectly circular","Roughly triangular"],"answer":3,"exp":"The human adrenal gland is roughly triangular in cross section. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex is composed of cells arranged in which configuration?","choices":["Radial columns","Ovoid configuration","Straight parallel cords","Loose meshwork"],"answer":1,"exp":"The zona glomerulosa is composed of cells grouped in an ovoid configuration. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"The zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex is composed of cells with a distinguishing cytoplasmic feature described as:","choices":["Densely eosinophilic, arranged in compact sheets","Basophilic cytoplasm, arranged in small nests","Lipid-droplet laden, arranged in radial columns","Clear vacuolated cytoplasm, arranged in a meshwork"],"answer":2,"exp":"The zona fasciculata is composed of lipid-droplet-laden cells arranged in radial columns, secreting glucocorticoids. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"The chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are considered modified versions of which type of neuron?","choices":["Postganglionic sympathetic neurons","Preganglionic sympathetic neurons","Somatic motor neurons","Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are considered modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons that lack dendrites and axons. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"The adrenal medulla contains a large vessel described in the lecture as the:","choices":["Medullary lymphatic trunk","Central vein","Peripheral sinusoid only","Central artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The adrenal medulla contains a large central vein. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"According to the lecture's overview, which of the following is listed among the 'main' endocrine organs, rather than an 'other endocrine tissue'?","choices":["Thymus gland","Adipose cells","Heart","Kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thymus gland is listed among the main endocrine organs, alongside the pituitary, hypothalamus, thyroid, suprarenal glands, pineal gland, parathyroid glands, pancreas, and reproductive glands. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 3–4"},{"q":"Per the lecture's overview figure, which hormone(s) does adipose tissue secrete as an endocrine tissue?","choices":["Insulin and glucagon","Natriuretic peptides","Erythropoietin and calcitriol","Leptin and resistin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adipose tissue is depicted as secreting leptin and resistin. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Per the lecture's overview figure, the gonads are depicted secreting androgens and inhibin (testes) or estrogens, progestins, and inhibin (ovaries). Which structure is grouped with these as producing 'numerous hormones detailed elsewhere'?","choices":["The digestive tract","The thymus gland tissue","The pineal gland tissue","The cardiac atria"],"answer":0,"exp":"The digestive tract is depicted as producing numerous hormones detailed elsewhere in the source text, alongside the gonadal hormones shown in the same overview figure. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"According to the lecture's overview figure key, ACTH stands for:","choices":["Adrenal cortical thyroid hormone","Antidiuretic corticotropic hormone","Anterior corticotropin-thyrotropic hormone","Adrenocorticotropic hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"ACTH stands for adrenocorticotropic hormone, one of the pituitary hormones listed in the figure key. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"According to the lecture's overview figure key, MSH stands for:","choices":["Medullary secretory hormone","Median-stimulating hormone","Melanocyte-stimulating hormone","Mineralocorticoid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"MSH stands for melanocyte-stimulating hormone, one of the pituitary hormones listed in the figure key. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
